{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.88,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": [
        "R1",
        "Q1",
        "Q2"
      ],
      "pins": [
        "R1.1",
        "R1.2",
        "Q1.1",
        "Q1.2",
        "Q2.3",
        "Q2.2"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "VCC",
        "Net-(Q1-G)",
        "GND"
      ],
      "observed": "R1 is a 10 Ω resistor connected between the VCC rail and the P-MOSFET gate node. That gate node is also connected to the collector of NPN Q2, whose emitter is tied to GND.",
      "expected": "A gate pull-up resistor for a high-side P-MOSFET driven by a small-signal NPN should normally be a high resistance, such as 10 kΩ, so that asserting the PWM signal sinks only a small current. A 10 Ω pull-up is about three orders of magnitude too low and can force excessive current through the 0805 resistor and the BC817 transistor.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R1 | 10 | Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric | Device:R_Small"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "Net-(Q1-G) : Q1.1(G,input) Q2.3(C,passive) R1.2(passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "VCC : C1.1(passive) J5.2(Pin_2,passive) JP1.2(B,passive) Q1.2(S,passive) R1.1(passive) R6.1(passive) U1.3(VI,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "R1 VCC Net-_Q1-G_ 10"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "When PWM turns Q2 on, the 10 Ω resistor pulls the P-MOSFET gate low through Q2, creating a large continuous current path from VCC to GND. At 5 V this is about 0.5 A, and at 12 V about 1.2 A, likely overheating or damaging R1 and Q2 and preventing normal fan control.",
      "confidence": 0.92
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files show a clear component-value anomaly: the P-MOSFET gate pull-up resistor R1 is 10 Ω instead of a high-value pull-up, creating an overcurrent path when the fan control transistor is enabled."
}

